The issue might be that my understanding of the buffer filling is incorrect. Let me reconsider the `fxUTF8Buffer` behavior more carefully.

Looking at `fxUTF8Buffer`:
```c
static txString fxUTF8Buffer(txParser* parser, txInteger character, txString string, txString limit)
{
        if (string + mxStringByteLength(character) > limit) {
                fxReportMemoryError(parser, parser->line, "buffer overflow");
        }
        return mxStringByteEncode(string, character);
}
```

The check is `string + mxStringByteLength(character) > limit`. For a single-byte character, `mxStringByteLength = 1`. So the check is `string + 1 > limit`, i.e., `string >= limit`.

When `string == limit - 1` (i.e., `p == q - 1`), the check is `(q-1) + 1 > q` → `q > q` → false. So no error, and the write happens at `q - 1`, advancing `p` to `q`.

When `string == q`, the check is `q + 1 > q` → true. So `fxReportMemoryError` is called, which longjmps out.

So after writing 65535 characters, `p = q`. The next character `&` triggers `fxGetNextEntity(parser, p, q)` with `p = q`.

